    <section class="trust"><div class="container trust-grid"><div class="trust-item"><strong>Residential construction</strong><span>New homes, additions, and major renovations</span></div><div class="trust-item"><strong>Light commercial</strong><span>Envelope scopes coordinated with the project team</span></div><div class="trust-item"><strong>Insulation systems</strong><span>Materials selected for each assembly</span></div><div class="trust-item"><strong>Air-sealing details</strong><span>Close gaps before finishes conceal them</span></div></div></section>

    <section class="section"><div class="container intro-grid"><div class="copy"><span class="eyebrow">Start before drywall</span><h2>New construction is the best time to get the envelope right.</h2><p>Open framing provides access to cavities, transitions, penetrations, and other details that become difficult to reach later. Planning insulation and air sealing before finishes are installed helps prevent missed areas, rework, and avoidable comfort problems.</p><p>EcoPower works with builders, owners, architects, and trade partners to understand the assemblies, sequence the work, and complete a clear scope around the project schedule.</p><ul class="check-list"><li>Better access to every cavity</li><li>Cleaner trade coordination</li><li>Fewer hidden air pathways</li><li>Consistent installation depth</li></ul></div><figure class="feature-photo"><img src="https://placehold.co/1000x1200/e9fbf4/0c2748?text=Open+Wall+Framing" alt="Placeholder for insulation installed in open wall framing"></figure></div></section>

    <section class="section section-soft"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Why plan it early</span><h2>A coordinated envelope supports the entire building.</h2><p>Insulation is only one part of performance. The best results come from connecting thermal, air, moisture, and ventilation details across the project.</p></div><div class="cards"><article class="card"><div class="card-icon">01</div><h3>Comfort from move-in</h3><p>Consistent insulation and air-sealing details help reduce drafts and uneven room temperatures.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="card-icon">02</div><h3>Reduced rework</h3><p>Address cavities and penetrations while they are open rather than disturbing finished surfaces later.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="card-icon">03</div><h3>HVAC coordination</h3><p>A better-defined envelope supports accurate load calculations and equipment planning.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="card-icon">04</div><h3>Moisture awareness</h3><p>Assemblies are reviewed with air movement, drying potential, and project conditions in mind.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="card-icon">05</div><h3>Code-ready details</h3><p>Work is coordinated around the applicable plans, specifications, inspections, and project requirements.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="card-icon">06</div><h3>Quality verification</h3><p>Visual review and available testing help document that the scope was completed as intended.</p></article></div></div></section>

    <section class="section materials"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Material options</span><h2>Use the right insulation for each assembly.</h2><p>One product is rarely the best answer everywhere. Final selections depend on plans, code, budget, access, moisture strategy, fire requirements, and performance goals.</p></div><div class="material-grid"><article class="material"><h3>Fiberglass</h3><p>Batts or blown fiberglass can provide an economical solution when cavities are prepared and installation quality is carefully controlled.</p></article><article class="material"><h3>Cellulose</h3><p>Dense-pack or loose-fill cellulose may be used in appropriate wall, ceiling, and attic assemblies.</p></article><article class="material"><h3>Spray foam</h3><p>Open- or closed-cell foam may be considered where the assembly, access, and project requirements make it appropriate.</p></article><article class="material"><h3>Rigid foam</h3><p>Continuous or targeted rigid insulation can support foundations, rim areas, and other designed assemblies.</p></article><article class="material"><h3>Mineral wool</h3><p>Mineral wool may support thermal, sound, moisture, or fire-performance goals in selected locations.</p></article><article class="material"><h3>Air-sealing materials</h3><p>Sealants, tapes, gaskets, membranes, and blocking are selected for the opening and surrounding materials.</p></article></div></div></section>

    <section class="section"><div class="container scope-grid"><figure class="builder-photo"><img src="https://placehold.co/1100x850/f5f4ec/0c2748?text=Whole-Building+Envelope" alt="Placeholder for whole-building insulation scope"></figure><div class="copy"><span class="eyebrow">Scope coverage</span><h2>Coordinate the places that matter before they disappear.</h2><p>EcoPower can develop or complete scopes across the building envelope, including details that cross between framing, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, roofing, and finish trades.</p><ul class="check-list"><li>Exterior framed walls</li><li>Attics and rooflines</li><li>Floors over garages</li><li>Rim and band joists</li><li>Crawlspaces and basements</li><li>Kneewalls and bonus rooms</li><li>Window and door transitions</li><li>Utility penetrations and chases</li></ul></div></div></section>

    <section class="section section-soft"><div class="container builder-grid"><div class="copy"><span class="eyebrow">Built for project teams</span><h2>One envelope partner from planning through closeout.</h2><p>We coordinate access, sequencing, scope changes, and inspection timing with the people managing the build. Clear communication helps keep insulation and air-sealing work from becoming a last-minute obstacle.</p><figure class="builder-photo" style="margin-top:26px"><img src="https://placehold.co/1100x750/1a4d8f/ffffff?text=Builder+Coordination" alt="Placeholder for contractor and builder coordination"></figure></div><aside class="builder-panel"><h3>What to send us</h3><ul><li>Architectural plans and relevant details</li><li>Project address and construction schedule</li><li>Specified R-values and material requirements</li><li>Energy-code or program documentation</li><li>Known alternates or value-engineering goals</li><li>Contact information for the project lead</li></ul><h3 style="margin-top:28px">What EcoPower can provide</h3><ul><li>Scope review and material recommendations</li><li>Clear proposal and installation assumptions</li><li>Coordination with adjacent trades</li><li>Progress communication and jobsite documentation</li><li>Final review and available verification testing</li></ul></aside></div></section>

    <section class="section" id="process"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Project process</span><h2>From plans to completed envelope.</h2><p>The exact sequence follows the project, but the core workflow stays clear.</p></div><div class="process"><article class="process-step"><h3>Share the project</h3><p>Send plans, location, schedule, assemblies, and performance requirements.</p></article><article class="process-step"><h3>Review the scope</h3><p>Identify materials, access needs, transitions, exclusions, and coordination points.</p></article><article class="process-step"><h3>Confirm schedule</h3><p>Coordinate installation windows with rough-ins, inspections, and drywall.</p></article><article class="process-step"><h3>Install and document</h3><p>Complete the approved scope and communicate field conditions or changes.</p></article><article class="process-step"><h3>Verify and close out</h3><p>Review completed work and provide applicable testing or documentation.</p></article></div></div></section>

    <section class="section section-soft"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Common questions</span><h2>New construction insulation, explained.</h2></div><div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item"><summary>When should EcoPower become involved?</summary><p>Earlier is better. Plan review before framing or rough-ins provides more opportunity to coordinate assemblies, penetrations, scheduling, and material choices. Installation is typically sequenced before drywall and after required rough inspections.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Do you work directly with builders and general contractors?</summary><p>Yes. EcoPower can coordinate with builders, general contractors, architects, project managers, homeowners, and relevant trade partners.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Can you handle both insulation and air sealing?</summary><p>Yes. Coordinating both scopes helps connect the thermal boundary with the air barrier and reduces gaps between separate trades.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Which insulation product is best?</summary><p>It depends on the assembly and project goals. Plans, code requirements, budget, access, moisture strategy, and performance targets all affect the recommendation.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Can you quote from plans?</summary><p>Yes. Send the available drawings, specifications, schedule, project address, and relevant energy or code information. A site meeting may still be needed before the final scope is confirmed.</p></details></div></div></section>

